WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.940
Start with the architecture.

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:04.100
Your agent writes a program.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:07.620
Your application selects a human-approved
blueprint and

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:09.460
supplies trusted context.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:13.380
Submilli provides the compiler and
execution runtime.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:17.380
The server is one way to host that
runtime, not the runtime

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:18.211
itself.

00:00:19.533 --> 00:00:23.533
Submilli implements its own parser, type
checker, and code

00:00:23.613 --> 00:00:26.973
generator for a stricter form of familiar
TypeScript.

00:00:27.533 --> 00:00:30.093
Types catch mistakes before execution.

00:00:30.573 --> 00:00:34.413
The compiler produces WebAssembly for an
isolated instance,

00:00:34.573 --> 00:00:37.853
using existing WebAssembly libraries and
engine technology

00:00:37.933 --> 00:00:38.681
underneath.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.560
Node and npm libraries target a general-
purpose host, without

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.040
Submilli’s operation-level checks.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:50.560
So generated programs use Submilli’s
standard library and

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:52.800
selected packages instead.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.480
Building the language boundary makes those
imports explicit;

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.240
package authors supply typed operations
and checks the runtime

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:01.316
can enforce.

00:01:02.633 --> 00:01:06.233
A package exposes operations in business
terms.

00:01:06.313 --> 00:01:09.913
Its author explicitly places a permission
check, passing

00:01:09.953 --> 00:01:12.553
the operation and relevant arguments.

00:01:12.593 --> 00:01:16.233
The runtime evaluates those facts against
the blueprint.

00:01:16.313 --> 00:01:19.193
A human creates and approves those rules;

00:01:19.273 --> 00:01:21.154
an agent may help draft them.

00:01:22.467 --> 00:01:25.507
Here, the application binds Customer A.

00:01:25.987 --> 00:01:29.427
The package asks to list charges for
Customer A, and the

00:01:29.507 --> 00:01:30.387
rule matches.

00:01:30.947 --> 00:01:33.987
Asking for Customer B fails that same
rule.

00:01:34.467 --> 00:01:37.987
These labels represent the quickstart’s
local fixture, not

00:01:38.067 --> 00:01:39.587
a live billing service.

00:01:39.827 --> 00:01:42.738
The program cannot choose its trusted
binding.

00:01:44.067 --> 00:01:45.907
The tradeoff is deliberate.

00:01:46.307 --> 00:01:49.667
You use the supported language and expose
integrations through

00:01:49.747 --> 00:01:52.947
Submilli packages or configured MCP tools.

00:01:53.507 --> 00:01:56.147
You give up drop-in npm compatibility.

00:01:56.707 --> 00:02:00.147
In return, the runtime gets a controlled
set of operations

00:02:00.227 --> 00:02:03.476
to check, while your application keeps the
agent loop.
